## 2. The family — two leaves, the /link leaf withdrawn

### [CXC-ERR-FAMILY] The family is TWO leaves — register (periodic STATE) and event (retained EVENT); /link is WITHDRAWN

- ruling: David ruled (2026-08-25) the family is `_error/<id>/register` + `_error/<id>/event`
  ONLY. A third `_error/<id>/link` leaf was proposed (transport/CAN health — TEC/REC,
  error-active/passive/bus-off, restart_count) and is WITHDRAWN: [CXC-ERR-FAULT-OWNERSHIP]
  closes it out, because a link/transport problem is a NODE FAULT under the fault-ownership
  rule, not a distinct telemetry leaf — it is a comms-class `_error/event` plus a `_register`
  comms bit, same as any other fault.
  Reference: `DEC-COMMON-ERROR-NAMESPACE-CONVENTION-2026-08-25` property `family-is-two-leaves`
  / `link-leaf-dropped`.
- dissolves-a-gap: this also dissolves composeio-expert's "CAN /link has no producer" framing —
  the producer to WRITE is a comms-class error EVENT on bus-off, never a `/link` state feed. See
  [CXC-ERR-GAPS] for what remains unbuilt.
- consequence: continuous link-health metrics (RSSI/SNR, TEC/REC counters, reconnect counts) are
  NOT `_error` data at all — see [CXC-ERR-LINK-METRICS-ARE-TELEMETRY].
- status: decided
- provenance: david-ruling
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-ERR-NAMESPACE]
- see-also: [CXC-ERR-REGISTER], [CXC-ERR-EVENT], [CXC-ERR-FAULT-OWNERSHIP]

### [CXC-ERR-FAULT-OWNERSHIP] MUST — the inclusion boundary is FAULT OWNERSHIP, not connector-vs-application

- rule: A fault the EMITTER/NODE owns — its own link, hardware, resource, config, or its own
  state — MUST be reported in `_error`, captured uniformly. A fault a REMOTE CALLER caused,
  returned to that caller through the protocol's own error mechanism, MUST NOT be reported in
  `_error`.
- corrected-exemplar: MCP endpoint-topology errors (not-bound, tool-not-found, wrong-tenant)
  were an earlier, MISLEADING exemplar of "connector errors stay out." They are
  caller-MISUSE returned to the caller — out, but NOT because they are "connector" errors; the
  dividing line was never connector-vs-application.
- usb-midi-and-lora-in: USB-MIDI errors (device disconnect, SysEx write fail, buffer overflow)
  and LoRa errors (link timeout, CRC, RSSI/SNR degradation, packet loss, join failure) are
  NODE/LINK faults and belong SQUARELY in `_error` (comms class, `_error/event`). This corrects
  an earlier architect over-broad lean that would have kept all "connector" errors out.
- mcp-connector-faults-in: the tell that confirms the boundary — even for MCP, the CONNECTOR
  faults (bind failure, socket down) ARE node faults and belong in `_error` (0x51
  `UNSAFE_BIND_REFUSED`, [CXC-ERR-SEED-V1]); only the caller-conversation faults stay out.
- status: decided — confirmed by David 2026-08-25, no longer architect-inference-pending-confirm
- provenance: david-ruling (the boundary itself, prompted by David's USB-MIDI/LoRa challenge
  which corrected the architect's over-broad lean); the "fault ownership" articulation as a
  named principle is architect-synthesis of David's intent, per [DX-INTERPRETATION-IS-AN-INFERENCE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-ERR-NAMESPACE]
- see-also: [CXC-ERR-FAMILY], [CXC-ERR-TAXONOMY]

---

## 3. The two leaves, in detail

### [CXC-ERR-REGISTER] `_error/<id>/register` — STATE, published PERIODICALLY; silence IS the death report

- shape: an 8-bit bitfield of currently-active error CLASSES (bit0 comms … bit7 internal, see
  [CXC-ERR-TAXONOMY]). One bit per CLASS, not per code — the register answers "what kind of
  thing is wrong," the paired `/event` answers "which specific thing, when, how many times."
- cadence: PERIODIC, not on-change, per [CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND]'s announcement-must-be-periodic
  rule — a missing publication IS the fault report; query cannot distinguish "healthy" from
  "did not hear." Fold into the existing 5-second heartbeat cadence rather than adding a new
  timer.
- latch: `UNKNOWN_TRANSPORT_TYPE` (0x21) and `UNSAFE_BIND_REFUSED` (0x51) are deliberately fatal
  by design — their register bit LATCHES until standup is re-attempted; it never self-clears on
  its own. See [CXC-ERR-SEVERITY-DEFAULT].
- status: proposed — mechanism specifics (bit layout, cadence fold-in) are architect-synthesis
  realizing David's ruled family shape
- provenance: david-ruling (the leaf exists, is STATE, is periodic — [CXC-ERR-FAMILY]); the
  8-bit-per-class bitfield mechanics are architect + can-bus-expert-recommendation, grounded in
  CANopen Error Register 1001h
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-ERR-NAMESPACE]
- see-also: [CXC-ERR-TAXONOMY], [CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND], [CXC-ERR-EPISTEMICS-GATE]

### [CXC-ERR-EVENT] `_error/<id>/event` — EVENT + retention:N; the discrete occurrence and its bounded history

- shape: an ordinary `event`-mode namespace (wire type `event`, never a distinct wire type —
  see the depends-on amendment) with `retention: N` set, per the collapsed namespace-mode model
  ([BP-EXT-LOG] §2.4). This IS 1003h/DM2's role: the discrete occurrence plus its bounded
  history, queryable via the same `GET /api/v1/log/{namespace}` path any retained-event
  namespace already exposes.
- payload: `{code, fmi, severity, source, ts, occurrence_count}` — `code` is the allocated
  register-taxonomy code ([CXC-ERR-TAXONOMY]/[CXC-ERR-SEED-V1]); `fmi` is a J1939-style Failure
  Mode Identifier refining `code`; `severity` is the per-occurrence value (see
  [CXC-ERR-SEVERITY-DEFAULT] — a default per code, not a fixed property of it); `source` names
  the owning subsystem/module; `ts` is receiver-side per [BP-MSG-FIELDS]'s `ts` discipline
  (never publisher clock — see `72-freshness-receiver.lts`); `occurrence_count` is a running
  tally, see [CXC-ERR-GAPS] for its unbuilt counting layer.
- status: proposed
- provenance: david-ruling (the leaf exists, is EVENT+retention — [CXC-ERR-FAMILY]); the payload
  field list is architect + can-bus-expert-recommendation, grounded in J1939 DM1/DM2's
  SPN/FMI/occurrence-count shape
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-ERR-NAMESPACE]
- see-also: [CXC-ERR-REGISTER], [CXC-ERR-SEVERITY-DEFAULT], [CXC-ERR-GAPS]

