## 5. Severity, arbitration, scope

### [CXC-ERR-SEVERITY-DEFAULT] Severity is a PER-OCCURRENCE DEFAULT, not a fixed property of the code

- rule: a code's `severity-default` (table above) is the value used when nothing overrides it —
  it is NOT a compile-time-fixed property of the code. `0x70 ENGINE_VALUE_REJECTED` is the
  worked example: warning on the external-dispatch path (log+drop, the value is refused and
  nothing crashes) but critical on the unguarded LOCAL path that can SIGABRT (empty-string into
  a binary domain check) — the SAME code, two different severities, because the consequence
  differs by call site, not by code identity.
- fatal-latch: `0x21 UNKNOWN_TRANSPORT_TYPE` and `0x51 UNSAFE_BIND_REFUSED` are DELIBERATELY
  FATAL by design and never take a lesser severity — their `_register` bit LATCHES (does not
  self-clear) until standup is re-attempted. This is the one exception to "severity is
  per-occurrence": these two codes are fixed-critical because there is no non-fatal
  interpretation of either condition.
- status: decided
- provenance: david-ruling (the two fatal-latch codes, the per-occurrence-default principle for
  0x70); architect-synthesis (generalizing the 0x70 worked example into the standing rule for
  every other code)
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-ERR-NAMESPACE]
- see-also: [CXC-ERR-SEED-V1], [CXC-ERR-ARBITRATION]

### [CXC-ERR-ARBITRATION] MUST — severity reaches the arbitration id, not only the payload

- rule: a payload-only severity arbitrates at one fixed tier regardless of how bad the fault is,
  so a critical fault would queue behind ordinary telemetry exactly when things go wrong. David
  concurred explicitly this is unacceptable.
- mechanism: declared-priority-at-announce promotes a critical device fault to tier 0
  ([BP-WIRE-PRIORITY]'s alarm tier), plus a DEFAULT rule — `_error/` -> `PRIORITY_LIVENESS`
  (tier 4) — so an ordinary error report out-arbitrates telemetry and display but never
  starves control, time-sync, or ACK.
- status: decided (the requirement and the default-tier rule); proposed (the declared-priority
  wiring mechanics, which depend on [CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND]'s announce mechanism landing)
- provenance: david-ruling (severity MUST reach arbitration; the PRIORITY_LIVENESS default);
  architect + can-bus-expert-recommendation (the declared-priority-at-announce mechanism)
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-ERR-NAMESPACE]
- see-also: [CXC-ERR-SEVERITY-DEFAULT], [BP-WIRE-PRIORITY]

### [CXC-ERR-SCOPE-DEFAULT] Scope defaults — critical/alarm global, warning/info local

- rule: critical and alarm severities default to GLOBAL scope — a fault of that severity MUST
  escape its own locality. Warning and info severities default to LOCAL scope — do not flood the
  shared segment with a chattering node's routine warnings.
- status: decided
- provenance: david-ruling
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-ERR-NAMESPACE]
- see-also: [CXC-ERR-ARBITRATION]

---

## 6. Capture

### [CXC-ERR-CAPTURE] ONE bus_scribe `errors` profile over `_error/*`, SEVERITY-PARTITIONED

- rule: a single durable `bus_scribe` capture profile named `errors` subscribes the `_error/*`
  prefix — the prefix is what makes one profile possible at all, instead of a hand-maintained
  per-emitter register that rots the way `CAN_BRIDGE_REGISTER` already has
  ([CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND]'s `evidence-hand-typed-registry`).
- partitioning: the profile MUST be SEVERITY-PARTITIONED so an info/warning burst cannot
  evict-oldest the critical record that started a cascade — an ordinary bounded ring buffer
  shared across severities would let exactly the noise around an incident erase the incident.
- status: proposed
- provenance: david-ruling (one profile, `_error/*`, severity-partitioned); [SPEC:CXC-BUS-SCRIBE]
  (`81-bus-scribe.lts`) is the mechanism this profile is declared against
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-ERR-NAMESPACE]
- see-also: [SPEC:CXC-BUS-SCRIBE]

---

## 7. Link telemetry is NOT error data

### [CXC-ERR-LINK-METRICS-ARE-TELEMETRY] Continuous link metrics are ordinary STATE telemetry; a fault enters `_error` only on threshold-crossing

- rule: RSSI/SNR, TEC/REC counters, reconnect counts and similar CONTINUOUS link-health metrics
  are ordinary STATE telemetry a node publishes on its OWN namespaces — they are NOT `_error`
  data. A fault enters `_error` only when a threshold crosses into an actual fault condition
  (then: a comms-class `_error/event` plus the corresponding `_register` bit).
- future-out-of-scope: a UNIFORM fleet-wide link-TELEMETRY convention, if ever wanted, is a
  SEPARATE future thing OUTSIDE `_error` — `_error` stays reserved for faults only. This is an
  architect note, not a David ruling.
- status: decided (the boundary itself, as a consequence of [CXC-ERR-FAULT-OWNERSHIP] closing
  the `/link` leaf); the future-telemetry-convention note is explicitly NOT decided
- provenance: architect-synthesis, drawn as the direct consequence of [CXC-ERR-FAMILY] /
  [CXC-ERR-FAULT-OWNERSHIP]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-ERR-NAMESPACE]
- see-also: [CXC-ERR-FAMILY], [CXC-ERR-FAULT-OWNERSHIP]

---

## 8. Epistemics gate — BLOCKING acceptance

### [CXC-ERR-EPISTEMICS-GATE] BLOCKING — no error convention ships without a negative control AND the periodic register

- rule: per [CXC-VISION-EPISTEMICS]'s `rule-negative-control` ("an instrument that can only
  report success is not an instrument"), this convention does NOT ship without BOTH:
  (a) a FAULT INJECTOR as negative control — an `_error` channel only ever observed empty is the
  drop-counter-pinned-at-zero failure, structurally incapable of proving anything; and
  (b) the PERIODIC register ([CXC-ERR-REGISTER]) — because death = silence, and on-change-only
  publishing makes a healthy node and a dead node indistinguishable.
- status: decided (the gate is blocking); NOT YET SATISFIED — see the evidence slots below
- provenance: david-ruling
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-ERR-NAMESPACE]
- see-also: [CXC-VISION-EPISTEMICS], [CXC-ERR-REGISTER]

### [CXC-ERR-PRE-INTEGRATION-QUESTIONS] Pre-integration questions — EMPTY until answered

- q1-injector-target: EMPTY — which subsystem hosts the first fault injector, and does it inject
  at the register bit, the event payload, or the underlying condition (e.g. actually pulling a
  CAN transceiver's supply to force LINK_DOWN)? Injecting at the condition is the only version
  that also exercises the real producer path; injecting at the register/event level only proves
  the capture pipeline.
- q2-file-line-grounding: EMPTY — every [CXC-ERR-SEED-V1] row marked "not independently
  re-grounded this pass" needs its file:line confirmed (or corrected) before a producer is
  written against it.
- q3-occurrence-count-source: EMPTY — [CXC-ERR-GAPS] names this as unbuilt; which layer owns
  incrementing `occurrence_count` (the emitter, the register/event helper library, or
  bus_storage on ingest)?
- q4-latch-clear-mechanism: EMPTY — "latches until standup is re-attempted" (0x21, 0x51) needs a
  concrete clear trigger: does a fresh `standup()` call clear the bit unconditionally, or only on
  confirmed success of the previously-failing step?
- status: draft — these MUST be answered before build, not discovered during it
- provenance: architect-synthesis, per [DX-SPEC-CARRIES-ITS-EVIDENCE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-ERR-NAMESPACE]
- see-also: [CXC-ERR-EPISTEMICS-GATE]

### [CXC-ERR-POST-INTEGRATION-EVIDENCE] Post-integration evidence slots — EMPTY until a real bench/negative-control run fills them

- slot-fault-injector-run: EMPTY — no fault injector has run against any producer yet. Record
  here: which code, which injection method, the register bit observed to SET and later CLEAR,
  the paired `/event` entry's fields.
- slot-periodic-register-observed: EMPTY — no periodic register has been observed live on a bus
  segment yet. Record here: interval measured vs. declared cadence, and a confirmed SILENCE
  case (node powered off) producing the expected downstream "missing heartbeat" verdict rather
  than a false-healthy read.
- slot-severity-partition-proof: EMPTY — no proof yet that a synthetic info/warning burst fails
  to evict a critical record from the `bus_scribe errors` capture. Record here: burst size,
  capacity, and the critical record's survival.
- slot-bridge-frame-count: EMPTY — the frame-count fixture confirming the log-forwarder deletion
  closes the double-emit (01-composeio-bus-protocol.smd §1.6.1 / [BP-EXT-LOG] §2.4) has not been
  run against this convention's own producers. Record the fixture name and its pass/fail here
  once it exists.
- why-empty-matters: "never run on hardware/against a real bus" MUST be a queryable property, not
  something a reader has to already know — an unfilled slot IS the record that this has never
  touched a bench. Per [DX-SPEC-CARRIES-ITS-EVIDENCE].
- status: draft — all slots EMPTY as of this spec's authoring
- provenance: architect-synthesis, per [DX-SPEC-CARRIES-ITS-EVIDENCE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-ERR-NAMESPACE]
- see-also: [CXC-ERR-EPISTEMICS-GATE], [CXC-ERR-PRE-INTEGRATION-QUESTIONS]

---

## 9. Gaps — real conditions, no producer yet; do NOT mint codes

### [CXC-ERR-GAPS] Known gaps — do NOT allocate a code until a real producer exists

- gap-can-link: CAN `/link` health (bus-off, error-passive, TEC/REC, restart_count) has NO
  producer in `transport_can.py` today. This is the single biggest gap and the strongest
  argument the (withdrawn) `/link` leaf idea existed in the first place — under the ratified
  two-leaf family, this becomes a comms-class `_error/event` on bus-off plus a `_register` bit,
  written in `transport_can.py`, not a new leaf. Ships aspirational-empty for CAN until that
  producer is built.
- gap-mqtt-link: MQTT `/link` has no counter state (`reconnect_count` / time-since-connect)
  today either.
- gap-freshness-stale: freshness/staleness is a namespace TREATMENT (`72-freshness-receiver.lts`),
  not an `_error` event, today. A future `SIGNAL_STALE` (FMI 9) is a plausible deferred
  allocation once that treatment needs an error-visible face — NOT allocated here.
- gap-storage-faults: storage/docstore faults live in the `bus_storage`/`document_store`
  SERVICES, which were not part of the swept core. Sweep those separately before seeding
  resource/storage-class codes.
- gap-occurrence-count: `occurrence_count` (the `/event` payload field) has no source today —
  recurrence-counting is a convention-layer job still to be built. See
  [CXC-ERR-PRE-INTEGRATION-QUESTIONS] q3.
- rule: NONE of the above license minting a new code. A code with no producer is exactly the
  aspirational-empty state this list exists to name explicitly rather than let happen silently.
- status: decided (as a list of known-unbuilt conditions); each gap's eventual code allocation
  is undecided
- provenance: david-ruling accept-all (composeio-expert sweep findings); architect-synthesis
  (the "do not mint" rule stated as a standing discipline)
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-ERR-NAMESPACE]
- see-also: [CXC-ERR-DEFERRED], [CXC-ERR-SEED-V1]

---

## 10. Dependency on the wire-protocol amendment

### [CXC-ERR-DEPENDS-ON-WIRE-COLLAPSE] B depends on A — an error IS a retained event

- rule: this entire convention is only well-formed because `_error/event` can be an ordinary
  retained `event` namespace rather than a bespoke wire type. That depends on the LOG_ENTRY
  retirement and the `{state,event_only,log}` -> `{state,event}`+`retention:int` collapse
  landing FIRST (`spec/01-composeio-bus-protocol.smd` §1.6.1,
  `spec/01-amendment-bus-extensions.smd` §2.4, both amended 2026-08-25). This spec does not
  restate that amendment; it depends on it.
- status: decided
- provenance: david-ruling
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-ERR-NAMESPACE]
- see-also: [CXC-ERR-EVENT]

